1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,024
You're falling.

2
00:00:01,024 --> 00:00:06,009
Feet first, head last, straight toward the darkest thing in the universe.

3
00:00:06,009 --> 00:00:12,907
Below you: a perfect sphere of nothing — blacker than black, bending the stars around it like a lens.

4
00:00:12,907 --> 00:00:15,775
Above you: the entire universe, shrinking.

5
00:00:15,775 --> 00:00:18,301
There is no air, no sound, no rescue.

6
00:00:18,301 --> 00:00:23,833
In a few minutes, you will cross a boundary from which nothing has ever returned.

7
00:00:23,833 --> 00:00:27,930
This is what really happens when you fall into a black hole.

8
00:00:27,930 --> 00:00:32,417
Before we begin the fall, one question — what do YOU think is inside a black hole?

9
00:00:32,417 --> 00:00:32,910
A portal?

10
00:00:32,910 --> 00:00:33,730
A crushed star?

11
00:00:33,730 --> 00:00:34,551
Nothing at all?

12
00:00:34,551 --> 00:00:39,640
Drop your answer in the comments, because by the end of this video, your answer might change.

13
00:00:39,640 --> 00:00:40,571
Now — let's fall.

14
00:00:40,571 --> 00:00:44,237
Right now you're still far away, where gravity feels almost normal.

15
00:00:44,237 --> 00:00:47,301
But something strange is already happening to your body.

16
00:00:47,301 --> 00:00:51,132
Your feet, closer to the hole, are being pulled harder than your head.

17
00:00:51,132 --> 00:00:55,861
This difference is called a tidal force, and near a black hole it becomes a nightmare.

18
00:00:55,861 --> 00:00:58,115
Your feet accelerate away from your head.

19
00:00:58,115 --> 00:01:02,569
You stretch — first like taffy, then like a thread, then thinner than any thread.

20
00:01:02,569 --> 00:01:05,924
Scientists have a technical name for this: spaghettification.

21
00:01:05,924 --> 00:01:13,182
It blows my mind that the universe has a force so extreme it can unravel a human body atom by atom, like pulling apart cotton candy.

22
00:01:13,182 --> 00:01:18,229
But here's the part that breaks my brain even more: time itself is slowing down for you.

23
00:01:18,229 --> 00:01:22,933
The deeper you fall, the slower your clock ticks compared to the outside universe.

24
00:01:22,933 --> 00:01:27,235
To a distant observer, you appear frozen at the edge, never quite crossing.

25
00:01:27,235 --> 00:01:33,372
But from your point of view, only seconds pass — while outside, stars are born and die, galaxies collide...

26
00:01:33,372 --> 00:01:39,624
Just think about this for a second: you could watch the entire future of the universe fast-forward above you.

27
00:01:39,624 --> 00:01:40,263
And then...

28
00:01:40,263 --> 00:01:41,017
you cross it.

29
00:01:41,017 --> 00:01:42,062
The event horizon.

30
00:01:42,062 --> 00:01:43,397
The point of no return.

31
00:01:43,397 --> 00:01:46,532
Here's the terrifying part: you wouldn't feel a thing.

32
00:01:46,532 --> 00:01:48,332
No flash, no barrier, no alarm.

33
00:01:48,332 --> 00:01:54,370
Just a quiet, invisible line in space — and the moment you cross it, every possible future leads inward.

34
00:01:54,370 --> 00:01:57,853
Every road you could take, every direction you could turn...

35
00:01:57,853 --> 00:01:58,956
ends at the center.

36
00:01:58,956 --> 00:02:00,930
You are now inside the black hole.

37
00:02:00,930 --> 00:02:03,426
And nobody outside will ever see you again.

38
00:02:03,426 --> 00:02:10,202
At the center waits the singularity — a point where all the mass of a dead star is crushed into zero volume.

39
00:02:10,202 --> 00:02:11,771
Density becomes infinite.

40
00:02:11,771 --> 00:02:13,465
Our equations of physics...

41
00:02:13,465 --> 00:02:14,406
simply give up.

42
00:02:14,406 --> 00:02:15,850
Einstein's math breaks.

43
00:02:15,850 --> 00:02:17,104
Quantum math breaks.

44
00:02:17,104 --> 00:02:23,253
It is the one place in the universe where the laws of nature, as we understand them, stop working.

45
00:02:23,253 --> 00:02:28,524
Whatever happens at the singularity is beyond every theory humans have ever written.

46
00:02:28,524 --> 00:02:33,484
Now let's pull back — because the black hole you just fell into is small compared to the real monsters.

47
00:02:33,484 --> 00:02:40,611
At the heart of our own Milky Way sits Sagittarius A-star: four point three million times the mass of our Sun, twenty-six thousand light years away.

48
00:02:40,611 --> 00:02:43,838
Every star in our galaxy, including our Sun, is slowly orbiting it.

49
00:02:43,838 --> 00:02:48,894
We live in the suburbs of a sleeping giant — and it has been quietly sitting there for billions of years.

50
00:02:48,894 --> 00:02:52,122
But TON six-eighteen makes our giant look like a pebble.

51
00:02:52,122 --> 00:03:00,251
This ultra-massive black hole weighs sixty-six BILLION times our Sun — so heavy that its light has traveled eleven billion years to reach us.

52
00:03:00,251 --> 00:03:04,287
When I first read that number, I honestly had to put the article down.

53
00:03:04,287 --> 00:03:05,728
Sixty-six billion suns...

54
00:03:05,728 --> 00:03:07,342
crushed into a single point.

55
00:03:07,342 --> 00:03:09,072
The universe doesn't do small.

56
00:03:09,072 --> 00:03:16,403
We're exactly halfway through this fall — and if your brain is melting a little, good, that means it's working.

57
00:03:16,403 --> 00:03:24,593
Quick one: if cosmic mysteries like this are your thing, hit subscribe — Cosmic Unknown explores a new one every single day.

58
00:03:24,593 --> 00:03:26,046
Now, back to the fall.

59
00:03:26,046 --> 00:03:30,999
Because here's a twist nobody expected: black holes are not entirely black.

60
00:03:30,999 --> 00:03:35,160
In nineteen seventy-four, Stephen Hawking proved they slowly...

61
00:03:35,160 --> 00:03:35,490
leak.

62
00:03:39,500 --> 00:03:40,597
Hawking radiation.

63
00:03:40,597 --> 00:03:45,653
At the edge of the event horizon, pairs of particles constantly pop into existence.

64
00:03:45,653 --> 00:03:51,684
Sometimes one falls in and the other escapes — carrying away a tiny bit of the black hole's energy.

65
00:03:51,684 --> 00:03:57,838
Over timescales so vast they make the age of the universe look like a blink, a black hole evaporates.

66
00:03:57,838 --> 00:03:58,508
Completely.

67
00:03:58,508 --> 00:03:58,812
Gone.

68
00:03:58,812 --> 00:04:02,163
Which raises a terrifying question: when it vanishes...

69
00:04:02,163 --> 00:04:04,478
where does everything it swallowed GO?

70
00:04:04,478 --> 00:04:10,129
That question is called the information paradox, and it has haunted physics for fifty years.

71
00:04:10,129 --> 00:04:15,289
Here's why it matters: in quantum physics, information can never be truly destroyed.

72
00:04:15,289 --> 00:04:19,834
Burn a book, and the smoke and ash still contain every word, in principle.

73
00:04:19,834 --> 00:04:22,844
But a black hole seems to erase things perfectly.

74
00:04:22,844 --> 00:04:23,335
My take?

75
00:04:23,335 --> 00:04:31,136
I think the answer will rewrite physics the way Einstein rewrote Newton — and whoever solves it gets a Nobel Prize on the spot.

76
00:04:31,136 --> 00:04:38,046
I remember exactly where I was on April tenth, twenty nineteen, when humanity saw one for the first time.

77
00:04:38,046 --> 00:04:49,693
The Event Horizon Telescope — a virtual telescope the size of planet Earth — captured the shadow of the black hole in galaxy M eighty-seven, fifty-five million light years away.

78
00:04:49,693 --> 00:04:52,786
A glowing ring of fire around perfect darkness.

79
00:04:52,786 --> 00:04:56,010
I stared at that blurry orange donut for an hour.

80
00:04:56,010 --> 00:04:59,498
After a century of math, we finally had a photograph.

81
00:04:59,498 --> 00:05:06,033
And here's what that photo taught us: black holes are not cosmic vacuum cleaners randomly eating everything.

82
00:05:06,033 --> 00:05:07,243
They are architects.

83
00:05:07,243 --> 00:05:16,078
Almost every large galaxy has a supermassive black hole at its center, and the mass of that black hole decides how many stars the galaxy can form.

84
00:05:16,078 --> 00:05:19,164
They sculpt galaxies the way a heart drives a body.

85
00:05:19,164 --> 00:05:24,428
Without them, the universe would look completely different — we might not exist at all.

86
00:05:24,428 --> 00:05:26,518
So could anyone survive the fall?

87
00:05:26,518 --> 00:05:32,660
For small black holes, absolutely not — spaghettification would kill you long before the horizon.

88
00:05:32,660 --> 00:05:36,903
But for supermassive ones, the tidal forces at the edge are gentle.

89
00:05:36,903 --> 00:05:40,576
You could cross the event horizon of Sagittarius A-star...

90
00:05:40,576 --> 00:05:41,146
and live.

91
00:05:41,146 --> 00:05:41,906
For a while.

92
00:05:41,906 --> 00:05:44,376
Of course, the singularity still waits.

93
00:05:44,376 --> 00:05:51,722
But physicists have a wild idea: in a spinning black hole, the math allows a tunnel — a wormhole — straight through.

94
00:05:51,722 --> 00:05:53,748
Probably it collapses instantly.

95
00:05:53,748 --> 00:05:54,318
Probably.

96
00:05:54,318 --> 00:05:55,838
But the math says maybe.

97
00:05:55,838 --> 00:05:57,940
Just think about this for a second.

98
00:05:57,940 --> 00:06:06,650
Everything we've described — the stretching, the frozen time, the evaporating darkness — comes from equations written by humans sitting at desks.

99
00:06:06,650 --> 00:06:08,573
Einstein never saw a black hole.

100
00:06:08,573 --> 00:06:10,134
Hawking never touched one.

101
00:06:10,134 --> 00:06:14,880
Yet their math predicted all of this decades before any telescope confirmed it.

102
00:06:14,880 --> 00:06:22,088
That, to me, is the most beautiful thing in science: the universe obeying rules we figured out with nothing but thought.

103
00:06:22,088 --> 00:06:27,657
And here's my honest opinion — the deepest mystery isn't what's inside a black hole.

104
00:06:27,657 --> 00:06:29,645
It's what black holes are FOR.

105
00:06:29,645 --> 00:06:42,175
Some physicists now argue that every black hole might birth a new universe on the other side of its singularity — that our own Big Bang was the inside of a black hole in some parent cosmos.

106
00:06:42,175 --> 00:06:43,368
We can't prove it.

107
00:06:43,368 --> 00:06:48,008
But it blows my mind that the darkest objects in existence might be...

108
00:06:48,008 --> 00:06:48,406
seeds.

109
00:06:48,406 --> 00:06:51,986
That destruction and creation could be the same thing.

110
00:06:51,986 --> 00:06:53,346
Back to you, falling.

111
00:06:53,346 --> 00:06:55,094
Your seconds are almost up.

112
00:06:55,094 --> 00:06:59,303
The light from the outside universe has redshifted into darkness.

113
00:06:59,303 --> 00:07:07,203
Your body, stretched across kilometers of curved spacetime, is approaching the singularity — the place where physics ends.

114
00:07:07,203 --> 00:07:13,161
For the outside universe, you froze at the edge forever, a ghost smeared across the horizon.

115
00:07:13,161 --> 00:07:13,808
For you...

116
00:07:13,808 --> 00:07:15,298
there is only the fall.

117
00:07:15,298 --> 00:07:17,564
And then — we genuinely don't know.

118
00:07:17,564 --> 00:07:22,529
If this journey into the dark gave you chills, you're in exactly the right place.

119
00:07:22,529 --> 00:07:29,088
Subscribe to Cosmic Unknown for a new cosmic mystery every day — tomorrow we chase something even stranger.

120
00:07:29,088 --> 00:07:33,869
And tell me in the comments: would YOU take the fall, if you could survive it?

121
00:07:33,869 --> 00:07:35,095
I read every answer.

122
00:07:35,095 --> 00:07:36,566
See you among the stars.
